* t92sleepwk.a -- CONF68K test t92: a sleep cut short by a signal reports* the ticks it did not use.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): F$Sleep's OUTPUT is "d0.l = Remaining number of* ticks if awakened prematurely", and its FUNCTION text says "the process is* activated before the full time interval if a signal (in particular S$Wake)* is received" (F$Sleep, page 1 - 53).** Single process: A$Set asks for S$Wake (1) after 20 ticks, then the process* sleeps 500. S$Wake is the one signal that needs no intercept routine --* it wakes a sleeper and does nothing else -- so nothing here depends on* F$Icpt working, which t72 and t85 already cover.** obs is 1000 if most of the request is still unused, plus 100 if the call* returned without error, plus 10 if anything at all was left, plus 1 if* less was left than was asked for: 1111.** A system with no clock cannot be asked this question at all: F$Alarm and a* timed F$Sleep both report E$NoClk there, so either one failing is a SKIP* carrying that code, never a FAIL. The manual's own CAVEATS for F$Sleep say* the clock must be running for a timed sleep.** The three cheap digits alone would be satisfied by a system that returned* the ticks it SLEPT (about 20) instead of the ticks remaining (about 480),* which is the confusion most worth catching, so the 1000 asks that more* than half the request came back. Half of five seconds is a deliberately* coarse band: the two answers are 24 times apart, and a band that loose* can only be crossed by a machine that failed to schedule this process for* two and a half seconds after its own alarm fired.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1FSleep set $0AFAlarm set $56ASet set 1SWake set 1NAPTICKS set 500ALARMTK set 20*EXPECT set 1111* psect t92sleepwk,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart moveq #0,d0 move.w #ASet,d1 moveq #SWake,d2 moveq #ALARMTK,d3 trap #0 dc.w FAlarm bcs.w noclock* move.l #NAPTICKS,d0 trap #0 dc.w FSleep bcs.s noclock move.l d0,d5 moveq #100,d6 tst.l d5 beq.s t92less addi.l #10,d6t92less cmpi.l #NAPTICKS,d5 bhs.s t92half addq.l #1,d6t92half cmpi.l #NAPTICKS/2,d5 bls.s t92v addi.l #1000,d6t92v cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.w ispass bra.w isfail** no clock: the claim cannot be exercised here, so report the code that said* so rather than a verdict nobody asked for.noclock moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6 lea skipstr(pc),a5 bra.w report* use verdict.i use report.i*idstr dc.b "RESULT t92 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0skipstr dc.b "SKIP",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  a sleep cut short reports the ticks left",0 ends